{"parentUuid":"0880510e-3380-4d11-b768-942a8f20876e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ad71ee75d5a04187e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BGtmEj77hX747tYdoTTxKE","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"grid placement optimality proof CP-SAT no-good cuts facility location\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver returning non-optimal solutions with status \\\"OPTIMAL\\\" · Issue #3639 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3639\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT model that most of the time solved to optimality, but in rare cases it is determined to be infeasible · Issue #4839 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4839\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT claims to find optimal solution to a MILP\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/4fcAuWiIUFA\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04B_advanced_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver Algorithm Description? · Issue #2073 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2073\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query. The search results provide information about CP-SAT solver usage, parameters, and performance characteristics from Google OR-Tools documentation and related resources. However, the search results don't contain specific information about:\n\n- Grid placement optimality proofs\n- No-good cuts in the context of grid placement or facility location\n- Theoretical proofs connecting these concepts specifically\n\nThe results do show that:\n\nCP-SAT employs Branch and Cut techniques, including linear relaxations and cutting planes, as part of its toolkit, and cutting planes such as Gomory-Cuts are applied to improve the linear relaxation.\n\nAdditionally, cuts are generated as part of CP-SAT's solving process, but the search results don't contain detailed information about no-good cuts specifically or their application to facility location grid placement problems with optimality proofs.\n\nIf you're looking for academic papers or more specialized technical documentation on these specific topics (grid placement optimality proofs, no-good cuts, and facility location), you may want to search academic databases or look for specialized constraint programming research papers.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"942e3e18-2629-4483-b1e9-f7fb50bc59b6","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:16.722Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0880510e-3380-4d11-b768-942a8f20876e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3b31ceb4-a2d0-48f0-b7b8-f160d0f8c4be","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ad71ee75d5a04187e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QFashC8f35p75LH5GcedG4","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Korf optimal rectangle packing branch bound symmetry breaking\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Optimal Rectangle Packing: Initial Results Richard E. Korf\",\"url\":\"https://cdn.aaai.org/ICAPS/2003/ICAPS03-029.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal Rectangle Packing: An Absolute Placement ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1402.0557\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal Rectangle Packing: New Results Richard E. Korf\",\"url\":\"https://icaps04.icaps-conference.org/icapspapers/ICAPS04KorfR.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal Rectangle Packing: A Meta-CSP Approach\",\"url\":\"https://cdn.aaai.org/ICAPS/2006/ICAPS06-010.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal Rectangle Packing: Initial Results - AAAI\",\"url\":\"https://aaai.org/papers/icaps-03-029-optimal-rectangle-packing-initial-results/\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal rectangle packing | Annals of Operations Research | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10479-008-0463-6\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal rectangle packing | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220462008_Optimal_rectangle_packing\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Optimal Rectangle Packing: Initial Results | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Optimal-Rectangle-Packing:-Initial-Results-Korf/90a5de6349bed23e94f23985867f79c87a68e4a6\"},{\"title\":\"A Scale Invariant Algorithm for Packing Rectangles Perfectly Stefan Hougardy\",\"url\":\"http://lifeware.inria.fr/BPPC/BPPC12papers/submissions/bppc12_submission_1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Optimal rectangle packing | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Optimal-rectangle-packing-Korf-Moffitt/2f2e26e9e7b1e86dd44e0c42c13d58f37d1d64dd\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I can provide you with information about Korf's work on optimal rectangle packing using branch-and-bound with symmetry breaking:\n\n## Overview\n\nRichard E. Korf developed methods to find an enclosing rectangle of minimum area that contains a set of rectangles with no overlap, addressing this NP-complete problem. He used an anytime branch-and-bound algorithm to solve it.\n\n## Key Techniques\n\nKorf's approach includes an efficient wasted-space lower-bound computation based on a relaxation of the rectangle-packing problem to one-dimensional bin packing, and a dominance condition based on empty strips that speeds up the backtracking algorithm. The combination of these two techniques improved performance by a factor of over 12,000 on the problem of size n = 16, from over a week to less than a minute.\n\n## Constraint-Satisfaction Formulations\n\nKorf presented two different constraint-satisfaction formulations of the rectangle packing problem: the first searches a space of absolute placements of rectangles in the enclosing rectangle, while the other searches a space of relative placements between pairs of rectangles. For problems where the rectangle dimensions have low precision, such as small integers, absolute placement is generally more efficient, whereas for rectangles with high-precision dimensions, relative placement will be more effective.\n\n## Symmetry Breaking\n\nA key aspect of Korf's approach involved developing techniques that exploit both the symmetry and geometry present in the rectangle packing domain. The research demonstrated that systematic approaches to breaking symmetries in the search space were crucial for improving algorithm performance.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"20ea949b-db04-412b-b946-650152af0b2a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:18.942Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3b31ceb4-a2d0-48f0-b7b8-f160d0f8c4be","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"45dd7001-f6da-4f9a-91f2-f75f3bc751a3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ad71ee75d5a04187e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013r1bQt7dm3kN6sg9sNTRqP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"orthogonal packing infeasibility cut Clautiaux Jouglet Carlier\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[PDF] A new exact method for the two-dimensional orthogonal packing problem | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/A-new-exact-method-for-the-two-dimensional-packing-Clautiaux-Carlier/f7d5f8c60972c18812925715f685ce8ae5d5659d\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ktnr/BinPacking2D: Exact solutions for two-dimensional bin packing problems by branch-and-cut · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ktnr/BinPacking2D\"},{\"title\":\"An Exact Algorithm for the Two-Dimensional Stage-Unrestricted Guillotine Cutting/Packing Decision Problem | INFORMS Journal on Computing\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/ijoc.2016.0708\"},{\"title\":\"A new constraint programming approach for the orthogonal packing problem | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/222432411_A_new_constraint_programming_approach_for_the_orthogonal_packing_problem\"},{\"title\":\"A new search procedure for the two-dimensional orthogonal ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.dil.univ-mrs.fr/~gcolas/packing/jmma-20022015.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Orthogonal Packing Feasibility, Two-Dimensional Knapsack Problems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-64403-5_5\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] A New Graph-Theoretical Model for the Guillotine-Cutting Problem | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/A-New-Graph-Theoretical-Model-for-the-Problem-Clautiaux-Jouglet/8755427c0e41da2b9a3b4a03895c1bb95eed9720\"},{\"title\":\"A New Search Procedure for the Two-dimensional Orthogonal Packing Problem | Journal of Mathematical Modelling and Algorithms in Operations Research | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10852-015-9278-z\"},{\"title\":\"A new exact method for the two-dimensional orthogonal packing problem | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/223389474_A_new_exact_method_for_the_two-dimensional_orthogonal_packing_problem\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A new exact method for the two-dimensional orthogonal packing problem\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/15186662/A_new_exact_method_for_the_two-dimensional_orthogonal_packing_problem\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about the work of Clautiaux, Jouglet, and Carlier on orthogonal packing and infeasibility cuts.\n\nClautiaux, Jouglet, Carlier, and Moukrim published a paper in 2008 titled \"A new constraint programming approach for the orthogonal packing problem\" in Computers & Operations Research.\n\nIn this work, they solve the 2D-BPP (two-dimensional bin packing problem) with a combinatorial Benders decomposition that is based on a model in which the two-dimensional items and bins are represented by their areas, and infeasible packings are imposed by means of exponentially many no-good cuts.\n\nThe no-good cuts are exponentially many and it is better to include only cuts corresponding to minimal infeasible subsets, and computing if a given subset belongs to the infeasible class is NP-complete, as it corresponds to the classical Two-dimensional Orthogonal Packing Problem (2D-OPP).\n\nThe basic decomposition scheme is enriched with a number of preprocessing techniques, valid inequalities, lower bounding methods, and enhanced algorithms to produce the strongest possible cuts.\n\nThis research represents an important contribution to solving orthogonal packing problems using infeasibility cuts as part of a branch-and-cut approach.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"88b0f517-b84f-4c51-96d9-456dc35ceae0","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:19.060Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45dd7001-f6da-4f9a-91f2-f75f3bc751a3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"faf26f3a-2d2e-488a-9058-05d955159613","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ad71ee75d5a04187e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T4m2TzHCu29K18jQNpUYuQ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"factorio satisfactory optimal layout proof community\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Steam Community :: Guide :: The Ultimate Layout Guide [Outdated - some of it]\",\"url\":\"https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=718716491\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal Layouts - Factorio Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forums.factorio.com/viewtopic.php?t=31498\"},{\"title\":\"Steam Community :: Guide :: Automation Factorio Guidebook (Layout Basic)\",\"url\":\"https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=926546927\"},{\"title\":\"Where can I find most efficient layouts for each ressource possible ? :: Satisfactory General Discussions\",\"url\":\"https://steamcommunity.com/app/526870/discussions/0/3729575504106221676/?ctp=1\"},{\"title\":\"Steam Community :: Guide :: Satisfactory Base-layout Guide\",\"url\":\"https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2151025387\"},{\"title\":\"Theoretically Ideal Factory Layout - Factorio Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forums.factorio.com/viewtopic.php?t=49561\"},{\"title\":\"[Top 10] Satisfactory Best Factory Layouts For Efficiency | Gamers Decide\",\"url\":\"https://www.gamersdecide.com/articles/satisfactory-best-factory-layouts\"},{\"title\":\"Best factory base layouts & locations in Satisfactory - Dexerto\",\"url\":\"https://www.dexerto.com/gaming/best-factory-base-layouts-and-locations-in-satisfactory-2897735/\"},{\"title\":\"Need tips on layouts :: Satisfactory General Discussions\",\"url\":\"https://steamcommunity.com/app/526870/discussions/0/2953753908229979591/\"},{\"title\":\"Satisfactory Endgame: Plan Your Mega Factory and Optimal Layout\",\"url\":\"https://www.4netplayers.com/en-us/blog/satisfactory/satisfactory-mega-factory-layout-planning-endgame/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about both Factorio and Satisfactory optimal layouts and community discussions, though there isn't a specific \"proof\" document about which game has the objectively optimal layout.\n\n## Factorio Optimal Layouts\n\nThe Factorio community discusses circuit-free solutions of basic factory-design to achieve optimal item-throughput, involving belts (balancers, crossings), inserters, chests, furnaces, and assembling devices with optimized production chains and compact design.\n\nFor large-scale item movement over short distances, bots completely outclass belts, especially when a variety of items must be handled, while trains outclass belts for longer distances, which is why experienced base builders consider bots+trains superior to using belts+trains.\n\n## Satisfactory Optimal Layouts\n\nSatisfactory is a game about efficiency, and to get efficiency takes proper planning—perfect factories are rarely made on the fly, they take planning and effort.\n\nOnce it's time to scale up, it's worth examining the most basic design blocks for a given system to optimize the number of components in each, which makes things significantly faster to build and makes ultra-late-game more viable as both FPS issues and total object limit get stretched longer.\n\nRather than a single \"proof\" of optimal layout, both games have community discussions where players share best practices and different optimization priorities (efficiency vs. space conservation, power consumption, throughput, etc.).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3978b85d-d9c9-4d13-96f7-98dc57e6a6df","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:23.935Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"faf26f3a-2d2e-488a-9058-05d955159613","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
