{"parentUuid":"c1a9cf3c-ce26-4a13-a2c2-b8f2556cb7c3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ad71ee75d5a04187e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015zBowGpgTEvZY2bUJbMxyq","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"dual feasible functions DFF 2D packing lower bound certificate\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Multidimensional dual-feasible functions and fast lower bounds for the vector packing problem - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0377221713006760\"},{\"title\":\"New data-dependent dual-feasible functions and lower bounds for a two-dimensional bin-packing problem: International Journal of Production Research: Vol 47 , No 2 - Get Access\",\"url\":\"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207540802426656\"},{\"title\":\"New lower bounds for bin packing problems with conflicts - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0377221710000718\"},{\"title\":\"New data-dependent dual-feasible functions and lower bounds for a two-dimensional bin-packing problem | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/239396145_New_data-dependent_dual-feasible_functions_and_lower_bounds_for_a_two-dimensional_bin-packing_problem\"},{\"title\":\"Using dual feasible functions to construct fast lower bounds for routing and location problems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X14002194\"},{\"title\":\"Discrete Optimization New lower bounds for bin packing problems with conﬂicts\",\"url\":\"https://www.math.u-bordeaux.fr/~fclautia/publications/EJOR-CDFF.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"New reduction procedures and lower bounds for the two-dimensional bin packing problem with fixed orientation - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305054805002765\"},{\"title\":\"Column generation for vector packing with bounds from ... - GERAD\",\"url\":\"https://www.gerad.ca/colloques/ColumnGeneration2016/PDF/ValerioCarvalho.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"New data-dependent dual-feasible functions and lower bounds for a two-dimensional bin-packing problem | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/New-data-dependent-dual-feasible-functions-and-for-Clautiaux-Moukrim/64881055c9ba5c033827f1d2db48bc8fd5257070\"},{\"title\":\"Multidimensional dual-feasible functions and fast lower bounds for the vector packing problem | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/274026467_Multidimensional_dual-feasible_functions_and_fast_lower_bounds_for_the_vector_packing_problem\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I can provide you with information about dual feasible functions (DFF) and their application to 2D packing lower bounds:\n\n## Overview\n\nDual-feasible functions (DFFs) are tools for computing fast lower bounds for optimization problems, and they are related to dual solutions of the column generation model, with there always existing a function leading to the optimal value yielded by this model.\n\n## DFF and 2D Packing\n\nThe concept of DFFs has been extended to the m-dimensional case, though several difficulties arise when generalizing from 1-dimensional DFFs, induced by the fact that item size comparisons must be done componentwise, and therefore monotonicity and superadditivity take on different meanings.\n\nFor 2D bin packing specifically, the two-dimensional bin-packing problem (2BP) consists of minimizing the number of identical rectangles used to pack a set of smaller rectangles, and lower bounds have been proposed based on the total area of the items after application of dual feasible functions (DFF).\n\n## Lower Bounds and Certificates\n\nDFFs represent a well established tool for deriving high quality lower bounds in very short times, and a well-known DFF lower bounding approach consists of using DFFs to rapidly generate feasible values for the dual variables of a Column Generation (CG) program. For any VP-DFF function, the bound is never above the column generation bound.\n\nThe search results indicate that DFFs serve as certificates for lower bounds in packing problems, as they provide feasible dual solutions to the column generation formulations used in cutting and packing optimization.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"cf682485-88f4-4e60-9c82-ae3bad78410d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:37.894Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c1a9cf3c-ce26-4a13-a2c2-b8f2556cb7c3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"340a7a71-f83e-4627-9268-c990a9dc9c4c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ad71ee75d5a04187e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SArpYYSpiabNxVZBGUMM2s","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"polyomino tiling impossibility proof coloring argument competition\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A New Algorithm Based on Colouring Arguments for Identifying Impossible Polyomino Tiling Problems\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4893/15/2/65\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A New Algorithm Based on Colouring Arguments for Identifying Impossible Polyomino Tiling Problems\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/358708880_A_New_Algorithm_Based_on_Colouring_Arguments_for_Identifying_Impossible_Polyomino_Tiling_Problems\"},{\"title\":\"arXiv:math/0012179v1 [math.CO] 19 Dec 2000 POLYOMINO CONVOLUTIONS AND TILING\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0012179\"},{\"title\":\"JOURNAL OF COMBINATORIAL THEORY, Series A 53, 1833208 (1990) Tiling with\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0097316590900574/pdf?md5=c55d3a6d39e78086d69136b7811ea554&pid=1-s2.0-0097316590900574-main.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Tilings∗ Federico Ardila † Richard P. Stanley‡ 1 Introduction.\",\"url\":\"https://math.mit.edu/~rstan/papers/tilings.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"#A73 INTEGERS 23 (2023) COLORING AND BOUNDARY INVARIANTS FOR POLYOMINOES\",\"url\":\"https://math.colgate.edu/~integers/x73/x73.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Mutilated chessboard problem - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mutilated_chessboard_problem\"},{\"title\":\"Tiling with Polyominoes. Polyominoes have been very popular in… | by Kavita Vaishnaw | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@kavita.vaishnaw/tiling-with-polyominoes-903a864f0224\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A New Algorithm Based on Colouring Arguments for Identifying Impossible Polyomino Tiling Problems\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/76274903/A_New_Algorithm_Based_on_Colouring_Arguments_for_Identifying_Impossible_Polyomino_Tiling_Problems\"},{\"title\":\"MOP 2007 Blue Group Tiling Yufei Zhao Tiling: Coloring and Weights\",\"url\":\"https://yufeizhao.com/olympiad/tiling.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found comprehensive information about polyomino tiling impossibility proofs using coloring arguments:\n\n## Overview\n\nCheckerboard coloring arguments are applied to polyominoes and target regions to prove that polyominoes do not tile the region, with this technique identifying impossible tiling problems using polyominoes of arbitrary shape.\n\n## How Coloring Arguments Work\n\nWhen none of the possible parity sums equals the parity of the target region, this is called a parity violation which implies it is impossible for the given polyominoes to tile the target region.\n\nA classic example is the mutilated chessboard problem: regardless of where a domino is placed, it covers one black and one white square, so 31 dominoes cover 31 black and 31 white squares, but the board has 32 black squares and 30 white squares, so a tiling does not exist.\n\n## Advanced Coloring Schemes\n\nMore sophisticated coloring can demonstrate impossibility in cases where checkerboard coloring fails—for example, using four colors to show a 10×10 board cannot be tiled with 1×4 rectangles, since any 1×4 tile covers an even number of squares of each color, but there are 25 squares of each color.\n\n## Computational Approaches\n\nRecent work gives systematic mathematical treatment of coloring arguments based on parity violations, converting the combinatorial problem into linear Diophantine equations and developing algorithms to find all possible parity violations of tiling problems.\n\n## Limitations\n\nRemoving larger numbers of squares with equal numbers of each color can result in regions with no domino tiling, but for which coloring-based impossibility proofs do not work.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7c7be5cb-09d9-486c-81ab-094f6b6fb2ea","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:39.005Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"340a7a71-f83e-4627-9268-c990a9dc9c4c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"cfa12bc5-660b-4199-a975-865cf12655ef","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eb903157-6eb9-4dd8-8710-2e428622c046","agentId":"ad71ee75d5a04187e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012rgFDscCvB9ea8V857Vi3F","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"logic-based Benders\" 2D packing nogood cut strengthening\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Computational Evaluation of Cut-Strengthening Techniques in Logic-Based Benders’ Decomposition | Operations Research Forum | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43069-023-00242-3\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/logic-based-benders-decomposition-lbbd-4e47b3ae-690a-44a4-9d80-a191b385b4e6\"},{\"title\":\"Speeding Up Logic-Based Benders Decomposition by Strengthening Cuts with Graph Neural Networks | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-53969-5_3\"},{\"title\":\"A Primal Decomposition Algorithm for the Two-dimensional ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1909.06835\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Cuts | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-45039-6_3\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-based Benders Decomposition for Large-scale Optimization J. N. Hooker\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/LBBDbookChapter.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Hierarchical Rectangle Packing Solved by Multi-Level Recursive Logic-based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20239\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition - John Hooker\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/encyLBBD.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Combinatorial Benders Cuts\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/combinatorial-benders-cuts\"},{\"title\":\"ULD Build-Up Scheduling with Logic-Based Benders Decomposition | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-60597-0_17\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found substantial information about logic-based Benders decomposition, nogood cuts, and cut-strengthening techniques, including applications to 2D packing problems.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n### Logic-Based Benders Decomposition (LBBD) Overview\n\nLogic-based Benders' decomposition (LBBD) is a generalisation of classical Benders' decomposition that removes the requirement of linear programming subproblems, extending this approach to problems where the subproblem is an optimisation problem of any form by making use of logical deductions from subproblem solutions to generate Benders' cuts.\n\n### Nogood Cuts\n\nTwo essential categories of cuts in LBBD are nogood (no-good) or exclusion cuts, which encode that the current master assignment leads to infeasibility in the subproblem. Logical deductions from solving a constraint program can be used to generate cuts in the form of no-good inequalities for addition to a mathematical optimisation problem, but no-good inequalities are typically weak—causing slow convergence of the LBBD scheme.\n\n### Cut-Strengthening Techniques\n\nCut-strengthening techniques have a significant impact on the computational effectiveness of the logic-based Benders' decomposition scheme, and while there have been numerous cut-strengthening techniques proposed, very little is understood about which techniques achieve the best computational performance. \n\nA computational study involving 3000 problem instances shows that cut-strengthening techniques that generate irreducible cuts outperform the greedy algorithm and the use of no cut strengthening.\n\n### Application to 2D Packing\n\nIn 2D bin packing, after an area-based master relaxes geometric overlap, the subproblem checks for feasible packing, with possible strengthening by restricting to minimal infeasible sets (MIS). To obtain new effective cuts, it is important to find the minimal source of infeasibility, that means finding the Minimal Infeasible Subset (MIS) of items that still makes a set not packable into a single bin.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"cc193712-7c80-4a5f-b40a-92a86fd30af0","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:06:40.325Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cfa12bc5-660b-4199-a975-865cf12655ef","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
